# =============================================================================
# lock/qmldir — the lock screen's one piece of shared state
# =============================================================================
# LockState is a SINGLETON: there is exactly one of it, and anything in the
# lock screen can reach it without being handed a reference.
#
# It has to be a singleton for the same reason GreeterState does, and for one
# more besides.
#
#   1. The lock screen draws one full-screen surface PER MONITOR, and all of
#      those surfaces are looking at the same one question: is this machine
#      locked, and what has been typed so far. Two copies of that on a
#      two-monitor machine is a bug you only find when you plug a second screen
#      in — and on a lock screen, "only found later" means "found by the person
#      who cannot get into their computer".
#
#   2. Things OUTSIDE the lock screen have to be able to lock the machine: the
#      Aquarius menu's "Lock Screen" row, the Super+L key binding, and the idle
#      watcher. A singleton is a name they can all say. None of them has to know
#      where the lock screen's windows are, or whether any exist yet.
#
# QML will not treat a file as a singleton on the strength of its `pragma
# Singleton` line alone — it also has to be named here. Forget this and the
# lock screen fails to start with "LockState is not a type", which is a
# confusing way to be told you did not write it down.
# =============================================================================

singleton LockState 1.0 LockState.qml
